  "content": {
    "id": "p18-q20-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "20",
    "prompt": "Suppose that $k\\ne0$. Show $kx^2+(k+m)x+m=0$ has one solution if $k=m$, and two solutions if $k\\ne m$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\Delta=(k-m)^2$. Hence there is one real solution if $k=m$, and two distinct real solutions if $k\\ne m$.",
      "worked": "Since $k\\ne0$, the equation is quadratic.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(k+m)^2-4km\\\\\\Delta&=k^2+2km+m^2-4km\\\\\\Delta&=k^2-2km+m^2\\\\\\Delta&=(k-m)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nIf $k=m$, then $\\Delta=0$, so there is one real solution.\n\nIf $k\\ne m$, then $\\Delta>0$, so there are two distinct real solutions.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
